verdicts.instead:
Measure hover and focus instead of listing them by hand. In TestEveryScreenIsDesigned, after the closed pass:
- enable DOM and CSS through tab.Call;
- get the nodeIds of the controls;
- call CSS.forcePseudoState with ['hover'], then ['focus-visible'], on each one;
- run assertion (d) again.
The Tab's reader drops the events this triggers, so nothing blocks. Keep the static rows as a backstop for unseeded states. If the static check stays the only guard, it must at least pair, within each :hover or :focus-visible rule, the colour it sets with the background the base selector sets.

verdicts.checked:
- Read devtools.go:
  - line 91: `loads: make(chan struct{}, 32)`, so an early load event is buffered, not dropped;
  - Navigate (256-285): it drains the channel, then waits for the next load;
  - Call (227-233): any CDP error reply calls t.Fatalf;
  - Eval (303-311): it runs Runtime.evaluate with awaitPromise, and a page exception is fatal.

verdicts.why:
The about:blank route holds. A Navigate from about:blank to `/reports/<slug>#feedback-sent` is a navigation to a new document, so a load event fires every time, including on the 375 pass.

The route marked 'preferred', a real browser feedback POST and its 303, has no way to wait for the load:
- Navigate does not drive it;
- tab.loads is unexported;
- the only tool left is to poll with Eval, as the design's refused-upload POST does.
A Runtime.evaluate that lands while the 303 navigation tears the document down gets a CDP error ('Execution context was destroyed' or 'Inspected target navigated or closed'). Call turns that into a fatal failure. So the preferred route fails intermittently, depending on timing. The refused-upload state in the same test has the same race.

verdicts.checked:
- Read the design's token names:
  - colour: --text, --text-muted;
  - type: --text-xs, --text-sm, --text-md, --text-lg, --text-xl, --text-2xl;
  - also --font-*, --leading*, --space-*, --radius*, --control-h.
- Compared the prescription's list of colour-bearing properties with the CSS properties that take colours.
- Checked how the prescription interacts with M-add-1's `border: 1px solid; border-color: var(--…)` and with the colour-word regex in comment_test.go:1193.

verdicts.why:
Three holes.

1. Inside :root, 'every custom property not on a short list of type and space tokens is a colour token'. The obvious way to write that list is by prefix (`--text-`, `--space-`, `--radius`…). That exempts --text-muted, a colour token, so it could be written as a bare `#5c5c66` with no dark value: the stray-literal class this check exists to close.

2. Outside :root, the property list is open-ended. It misses:
- the text-decoration shorthand (`text-decoration: underline black`);
- text-shadow, column-rule, scrollbar-color;
- -webkit-text-fill-color, -webkit-tap-highlight-color;
- fill and stroke.
All of these take named colours that stay fixed when the scheme flips.

3. 'Colour part' needs a tokenizer. If the value of a colour-bearing property is checked as a whole, it rejects M-add-1's `border: 1px solid` and values like `background-repeat: no-repeat`, so the two prescriptions clash unless this is spelled out.
